{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"=== BLOCKING DISCOVERY BEFORE IMPLEMENTATION: THIS TICKET'S CENTRAL PREMISE NO LONGER HOLDS. ===\nClaimed and investigated; NOT implemented, because implementing the plan as written would ship\n~20 annotations that enforce NOTHING. Recording the measurement rather than proceeding.\n\nTHE TICKET'S PROOF DOES NOT REPRODUCE. Its three-way probe asserts that\n`def f(client: TicketTransport)` calling an undeclared member yields\n`error: \"TicketTransport\" has no attribute ...  [attr-defined]`. Run today under the repo's own\nsettings (`mypy src/rebar`, i.e. exactly what `make typecheck` runs — every file NAMED on the\ncommand line, which matters because `follow_imports = \"silent\"` reports only on named files), it\nproduces **NO ERROR AT ALL**. Not for a real member, and not for `definitely_not_a_member`.\n\nTHE CAUSE, ISOLATED WITH A CONTROLLED PROBE. Two functions in one file, one parameter each:\n\n    class PlainPort(Protocol):\n        def get_issue(self, remote_id: str) -> dict: ...\n\n    def via_metaclass_protocol(client: TicketTransport) -> None:\n        client.definitely_not_a_member(\"x\")      # -> NO ERROR\n    def via_plain_protocol(client: PlainPort) -> None:\n        client.definitely_not_a_member(\"x\")      # -> error: \"PlainPort\" has no attribute ...\n\nOnly the plain Protocol errors. The difference is\n`class TicketTransport(Protocol, metaclass=_TransportPortMeta)` (`_backend.py:184`, with\n`# type: ignore[misc]` on the metaclass at `:127`) — **the custom metaclass makes mypy stop\nattribute-checking the annotated type.** The `type: ignore[misc]` is the visible symptom: mypy\ndoes not model this construct, and silently degrades the annotated parameter rather than\ncomplaining at the use site.\n\nWHY THIS IS THE MOST IMPORTANT THING ON THE TICKET. The plan's whole thesis is \"annotate as the\nPORT, not as `Any`, because `Any` satisfies the ratchet while staying blind\". Against the current\n`TicketTransport`, **annotating as the port is ALSO blind** — it just looks rigorous. That is the\nsame failure the ticket was written to prevent, arriving by a route the ticket did not anticipate,\nand it would be invisible in review: the diff would show twenty correct-looking annotations and\n`make typecheck` would stay green because it was never going to say anything.\n\nWHERE THE METACLASS CAME FROM, so it is not removed casually. `_TransportPortMeta` is J9's\n([rebar:bda8-370d-23ad-4e36]) and its docstring gives the reason: `typing`'s own `isinstance`\nagainst a runtime-checkable Protocol goes through `ABCMeta`'s subclass CACHE, which memoises the\nfirst positive answer, so a class that conformed once keeps reporting `True` after a member is\nremoved — fatal for a guard whose job is detecting a missing member. The metaclass makes the\nRUNTIME check uncached and therefore honest. It is load-bearing for `assert_transport_conforms`.\n\nSO THE REAL DECISION THIS TICKET MUST MAKE — and it is a design fork, not an implementation\ndetail:\n  (a) give mypy a checkable view while keeping the runtime guard — e.g. declare the Protocol\n      PLAINLY and attach the uncached-`isinstance` behaviour to a separate runtime subclass or a\n      standalone conformance function, so static and runtime checking do not compete for the same\n      class object; or\n  (b) accept STATIC blindness and rely on J9's runtime `assert_transport_conforms()` alone, in\n      which case this ticket's premise is retired and it closes as not-a-bug with the finding\n      recorded — the annotations would still be worth doing for readability and for Serena/Pyright\n      navigation (bug dbf9), but NOT as an enforcement mechanism, and the plan must stop claiming\n      they are.\nEither way the plan needs rewriting before code is written; (a) is the substantive option and\nneeds its own verification that the split does not weaken the runtime guard bda8 added.\n\nSTATUS: claimed, plan certified, NOT implemented. Nothing pushed. The working tree is clean — the\nprobe file was removed after each measurement (`git status --porcelain` empty, verified)."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785628586587910001,"uuid":"0d3e9eaf-6c10-447a-8edf-9a08b29d7ef3"}